{
  "image_name": "4d07d0c2ddb307cd910efe85fca61d34.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure explains how crystal mirror symmetry constrains spin–orbit torque (SOT) directions. Its central message is that in a symmetric material, some spin polarizations are forbidden by symmetry while others are allowed.\n\nThe top-left sketches compare an original crystal picture with the same system rotated by 180° about the z axis. When the spin points perpendicular to the plane, the rotated version gives an incompatible result, marked as a contradiction, so that out-of-plane spin component is not allowed. In contrast, an in-plane spin direction remains symmetry-compatible after rotation.\n\nThe middle diagrams clarify why this happens using mirror operations: electric current changes sign under mirror inversion in a particular way, while spins and magnetic fields transform differently depending on whether they lie in the plane, point out of the page, or are normal to the mirror. Because these quantities invert differently, only certain current-induced spin directions can survive the symmetry.\n\nThe bottom atomic schematics connect this abstract rule to real structures. If current flows parallel to a structural mirror plane, perpendicular spins are forbidden. If a material has only one structural mirror plane and that plane is perpendicular to the current, perpendicular spins become allowed.",
